== Tube transducer excitation
<sec:methods_tube_excitation>
A signal generator (DG4102, RIGOL Technologies, Beijing, China), shown
in @fig:platform~(a) equipment item~5, was used to generate a
continuous $16.79 med upright("k") upright("Hz")$ sinusoidal voltage
signal of $2 med upright("s")$ duration, that was input to a
$+ 55 med upright("db")$ power amplifier (1040L, Electronics and
Innovation, USA) (@fig:platform~(a) equipment item~6) to excite
the tube transducer to generate a sonication. The $50 med upright("Ω")$
output of the amplifier was matched to the tube transducer by an
electrical impedance matching transformer (B66397G0000X187, ETD59/31/22,
N87, TDK EPCOS, Germany, equipment item 7) with a 1:5.7 turns ratio. A
separate $1 med upright("V")$ square wave signal triggered the
high-speed camera (@fig:platform~(a) equipment item~4). A range
of tube transducer input powers, determined by the voltage amplitude of
the $16.79 med upright("k") upright("Hz")$ sinusoid set on the signal
generator, were tested during preliminary experiments. Two powers were
selected to provide an equitable comparison with the sonotrode
(described below, section~@sec:methods_sonotrode) and to demonstrate the
capabilities of the tube transducer. These are $55 med upright("W")$, to
match the sonotrode input power used exclusively throughout this work
and $106 med upright("W")$, the maximum tube transducer input power
achievable by the equipment when the tube is filled with deionised
water. The input power measurement method is detailed in
section~@sec:experimental_procedure.

== Sonotrode
<sec:methods_sonotrode>
The cavitation generation and sonoprocessing performance of the tube
transducer was compared to that of a commercial sonotrode (P100, Sonic
Systems Ltd, UK), represented schematically in @fig:platform~(e &
f). This sonotrode has an operating frequency of
$20 med upright("k") upright("Hz")$ and a tip diameter of
$20 med upright("m") upright("m")$ was used, matching that of Lei
#emph[et al.] @lei2021lithium@lei2024effect. Sonotrode sonications were
studied in two different reservoir types. The first, shown in
@fig:platform~(e), was used for the sonoprocessing of intact LiB
anode sections. The second was a cylindrical vessel, with dimensions
matching that of the tube transducer (see
section~@sec:methods_tube_transducer) and shown in
@fig:platform~(d & f), used for the sonication of anode flakes to
provide an equitable comparison of the capabilities of each transducer
type. An aperture in the wall of the vessel receives the tip of the
sonotrode, with three vertical tip positions tested as represented by
coloured arrows in @fig:platform~(f): aligned to the top, centre
and bottom of the vessel, approximately
$5 med upright("m") upright("m")$ above the lowest point.

For this system, the tip displacement amplitude is user selected and
maintained during the sonication, irrespective of the acoustic load,
while electrical power from the driver is automatically varied to meet
the displacement amplitude demands. Transducer displacement from
$0 med upright("μ") upright("m")$ to $16 med upright("μ") upright("m")$
can be selected, corresponding to transducer electrical input powers
between $0 med upright("W")$ to $120 med upright("W")$, according to the
manufacturer's specifications. For this work, the maximum electrical
input power that was attained for all sonotrode sonications in deionised
water, was measured to be $55 med upright("W")$.

== Lithium-ion battery (LiB) anode samples
<sec:methods_LiB>
#figure(image("source/fig3_LiB_sample.png"),
  caption: [
    \(a)~A complete LiB anode sheet. (b)~An intact LiB anode section
    mounted within a bracket. (c)~LiB anode flakes with an effective
    area of $17 med upright("c") upright("m")^2$.
  ]
)
<fig:LiB>

Complete LiB anode sheets measuring approximately
$23 times 20 #h(0em) upright("cm")$, composed of a
$15 med upright("μ") upright("m")$ thick copper foil current collector
coated on both sides with $70 med upright("μ") upright("m")$ layer of
active material, @fig:LiB~(a), were sourced from unused Nissan
Leaf batteries. The active material coat is composed of graphite (with
an average particle diameter of $15 med upright("μ") upright("m")$) and
#emph[ca]. 4.5 wt% CMC/SBR binder @lei2021lithium. The surface density
of the LiB anode was measured as
$0.030 med upright("g") upright("/") upright("c") upright("m")^2$ using
high precision scales (PCE-BSK~310, PCE Instruments) with an accuracy of
$0.001 med upright("g")$.

The experimental studies of Lei #emph[et al.] @lei2024effect show that a
sonotrode with a $20 med upright("m") upright("m")$ diameter tip at a
distance of $5 med upright("m") upright("m")$ from the surface of an
intact LiB cathode section, removes graphite coating from a circular
area with a diameter of approximately
$10 med upright("m") upright("m")$, from the side of the cathode facing
the tip. For the current work, an intact section of LiB anode was held
within a mounting bracket, @fig:LiB~(b), and suspended
horizontally beneath either the sonotrode tip, @fig:platform~(e),
or across the centre of the tube transducer, @fig:platform~(c).
Within the bracket the anode section has an exposed area of
$3 times 3 #h(0em) upright("cm")$, with the section cut marginally
larger for secure mounting. The sonotrode was operated at
$55 med upright("W")$ for $2 med upright("s")$. for tip-to-anode
distances of $15 med upright("m") upright("m")$ and
$5 med upright("m") upright("m")$. The effects of tube transducer
sonication on the intact section, when operating at
$55 med upright("W")$ and $106 med upright("W")$ input powers for
$2 med upright("s")$, were also tested. Five intact anode sections were
sonicated for each of these four transducer configurations (two
sonotrode tip-to-anode distances and two tube transducer input powers),
as presented in #emph[Results] section~@sec:results_lib_section.

In an attempt to reduce the dependence of sonoprocessing effects on the
spatial distribution of the cavitation activity generated by the two
transducer types used in this study, we also assessed the graphite
coating delamination performance with LiB anode flakes, as seen in
@fig:LiB~(c). A number of approaches to material recovery from
end-of-life LiB anodes include mechanical crushing or shredding,
suggesting the use of flakes is both valid and of practical interest
@gaines2014future@kosenkoReviewExistingStrategies2023. Batches of flakes
of length and width less than $2 med upright("m") upright("m")$ were cut
from anode sheet, @fig:LiB~(c). Flake batches of effective areas
up to $24 med upright("c") upright("m")^2$ were tested, as detailed in
#emph[Results] section~@sec:results_effect_area. Graphite coating
removal was assessed by weighing dry anode flakes from each batch before
and after each $2 med upright("s")$ sonication. Five sample batches were
tested for each of the five transducer configurations investigated (the
sonotrode with three different tip positions and the tube transducer at
the two input powers), with data presented as the average $plus.minus$
standard deviation. Each batch of flakes was added to the water within
either the bore of the tube transducer or the cylindrical vessel (prior
to tip insertion), and allowed to settle to the bottom before the
sonication was initiated, as can be seen in
figures~@fig:camera_image_4cm2 and @fig:camera_image_17cm2.

== Experimental procedure
<sec:experimental_procedure>
Deionised water at room temperature was the host liquid medium for all
investigations (with the exception of the SCL study, detailed in
section~@subsec:methods_SCL). Any observed differences in cavitation
intensity or graphite coating delamination performance can therefore be
attributed to the transducer configuration or operating conditions.

Electrical power input into the tube transducer and sonotrode were
measured by using a digital multimeter (117 TRUE RMS, Fluke, USA). The
setup involves connecting the digital multimeter between the tube
transducer and the transformer, and between the sonotrode and the
control panel.

Post-sonication imaging of intact anode sheet sections and batches of
flake samples were taken with a surface microscope (SMZ-171, Motic,
China; maximum magnification $times 50$).

== Optical imaging
<sec:methods_imaging>
=== High-speed imaging (HSI)
<subsec:methods_HSI>
HSI of the cavitation inside the tube transducer and the cylindrical
sonotrode vessel during a sonication was captured with a FASTCAM SA-Z
2100~K (Photron, Bucks, UK) high-speed camera, @fig:platform~(a)
equipment item~4. The high-speed camera recorded the cavitation activity
for the entire $2 med upright("s")$ duration of each sonication at
$20000 #h(0em) upright("frames/s")$, through a macro-lens (Milvus 100~mm
f/2M, Zeiss, Oberkocken, Germany). At this frame rate, imaging was
achieved over $1024 times 1024$ pixels, with a resolution of
$54 #h(0em) upright("?m/pixel")$. Dual illumination fixtures, shown in
@fig:platform~(a) equipment items~2 and 3, ensured full
illumination of the tube transducer and cylindrical vessel interiors.
The peripheral regions were illuminated by a $150 med upright("W")$
halogen lamp (Thorlabs, Ely, UK) coupled with a fibre ring illuminator
(FRI61F50, Thorlabs). Central regions were irradiated by synchronous
$10 med upright("n") upright("s")$ laser pulses at
$640 med upright("n") upright("m")$ (CAVILUX Smart, Cavitar, Finland),
coupled to a liquid light guide with a collimating lens attached to the
output end.

=== Sonochemiluminescence (SCL) observation
<subsec:methods_SCL>
SCL imaging was employed to qualitatively assess cavitation activity
inside the tube transducer and the cylindrical sonotrode vessel.
Sonications for each of the five transducer configurations (see
sections~@sec:methods_tube_excitation and @sec:methods_sonotrode) were
undertaken with the SCL working solution---$1 med upright("l")$
deionised water with 0.1~M NaOH and $0.17 med upright("g")$ of luminol
@lei2024effect --- in a dark room. Sonication causes luminescent phenol to
react with hydroxyl radicals created by sonolysis to produce
3-aminophthalate, which emits blue light @wang2022ultrasound. It is
noted that although SCL indicates activity associated with the
generation of reactive species, this also indirectly indicates the
intensity of local cavitation activity @lei2024effect. To generate
sufficient luminescence, sonications of $10 med upright("s")$ durations
were necessary. Images were captured using a Nikon~Z8 digital camera
equipped with a NIKKOR~Z 24-120~mm f/4~s lens with a
$10 med upright("s")$ exposure time; #emph[f]/4 aperture and 3200~ISO.
No post-processing was applied to the captured SCL images.

Consequently, the original $24 #h(0em) upright("bit")$ true colour RGB
images were converted to $8 #h(0em) upright("bit")$ greyscale images
using the `rgb2gray` function in MATLAB
@themathworksinc.Rgb2grayConvertRGB. There were no instances of 0~value
greyscale levels throughout these converted images and so a filter was
created in Photoshop (Adobe Inc., San Jose, CA, USA) that assigned a
0~value to pixels corresponding to the transducers, vessels and exterior
areas (the non-liquid areas that do not emit SCL). Greyscale level
histograms of these altered images were calculated using the `histogram`
function in MATLAB (R2025a, MathWorks, MA, USA) and the values
corresponding to the 0~level were nullified
@themathworksinc.HistogramHistogramPlot. The histogram therefore
represents the prevalence of $2^8 - 1 = 255$ possible greyscale levels
within the cylindrical sonotrode vessel and the tube transducer, in the
processed versions of @fig:SCL~(a)--(e). The magnitude and
prevalence of these greyscale levels correspond to the intensity and
distribution of SCL, which in turn may be taken as an indicator of the
intensity of the cavitation activity
@tiongComparisonSonochemiluminescenceImages2017.

=== Conventional imaging of graphite coating removal
<subsec:methods_camera_imaging>
Conventional video imaging at $120 #h(0em) upright("frames/s")$ captured
the graphite coating delamination from LiB flakes within the tube
transducer and the cylindrical sonotrode vessel, during sonications.
Again, a Nikon~Z8 digital camera equipped with a NIKKOR~Z 24-120~mm
f/4~s lens was used. Imaging was undertaken over $3840 times 2160$
pixels with 1/125~$upright("s")$ exposure time, #emph[f]/4 aperture and
3200~ISO. Example video data from both HSI and conventional imaging are
available as #emph[Supplementary Materials].

= Results
<results>
The following results sections are organised to compare the cavitation
generated by the tube transducer and the sonotrode. Their effectiveness
in delaminating graphite coating from LiB anode, as intact sections and
in flake form, is evaluated for the various sonication configurations
described above.

- Section~@sec:results_hsi presents HSI results of cavitation activity
  generated by the sonotrode at $55 med upright("W")$, with the tip at
  three vertical positions within a cylindrical vessel, and by the tube
  transducer at two input powers ($55 med upright("W")$ and
  $106 med upright("W")$). This allows a comparison of the bubble
  distribution and structures that develop, during sonications with each
  configuration.

- Section~@sec:results_scl evaluates the spatial distribution and
  intensity of the cavitation activity via SCL imaging through
  qualitative observations and quantitatively, with greyscale histogram
  analysis.

- Section~@sec:results_lib_section presents the graphite coating
  delamination observations for the sonotrode and the tube transducer on
  intact LiB anode sections. These sonotrode sonications were undertaken
  in the tank of @fig:platform~(e), not the cylindrical vessel,
  such that the mounted anode section occupied a plane below the
  sonotrode tip, for comparison to the results of Lei #emph[et al.]
  @lei2024effect. Results for two tip-to-anode distances
  ($5 med upright("m") upright("m")$ and
  $15 med upright("m") upright("m")$) and the two input powers to the
  tube transducer are described.

- Section~@sec:results_lib_flake presents the observations of graphite
  coating removal from LiB anode flakes, with an effective area of
  $4 med upright("c") upright("m")^2$, for each of the five transducer
  configurations investigated by the optical imaging techniques,
  sections~@sec:results_hsi and @sec:results_scl. The results represent
  the conventional imaging taken during sonications and a comparison of
  post-sonication "remaining mass percentage".

- Section~@sec:results_effect_area explores the effect of increasing
  anode flake effective area loading on the graphite coating
  delamination capability of the tube transducer, at
  $106 med upright("W")$ input power. A threshold is identified, beyond
  which delamination efficiency reduces due to overloading. Conventional
  imaging of graphite coating delamination and percentage remaining mass
  data, for this threshold effective area load are finally presented in
  section~@sec:results_lib_17cm2.

